        # Since k is small (<= 25), we can iterate over all possible last elements and counts of changes.
        # We'll use dp[i][j] = max length of a good subsequence ending at index i with exactly j changes.
        # Transitions:
        #   For each i from 0 to n-1:
        #     For each j from 0 to k:
        #       Option 1: Extend a sequence ending at i-1 with same value and j-1 changes (if j>0)
        #       Option 2: Start new sequence at i with 0 changes (length 1)
        #       Option 3: If nums[i] == nums[i-1], we can extend with same j (no extra change)
        #       If nums[i] != nums[i-1], then extending from i-1 would cost one more change.
        #
        # Actually, a better state: 
        # dp[i][j] = max length of a good subsequence ending at index i with exactly j changes (in the subsequence)
        # To compute dp[i][j]:
        #   - We can always start a new sequence at i: length 1, j=0
        #   - Or extend a previous sequence ending at some p < i:
        #        if nums[p] == nums[i]: then new changes count = same as previous
        #        if nums[p] != nums[i]: then new changes count = previous + 1
        #
        # But iterating over all p is O(n^2 * k) which is acceptable since n<=500, k<=25.

        INF = -1
        dp = [[INF] * (k + 1) for _ in range(n)]

